From fa0fd38c69e9e22dcb54f08ac1456cfcf0257a33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 20:43:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 69/81] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, the zero-copy acquire refuses a
 deadlock too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: the copy fallback has refused to block since 0053 when every image
 of the swapchain is already with the application — only a present can
 free one there, so waiting is a deadlock and not a wait. The zero-copy
 path has the same condition and did not check it: a slot the
 compositor holds is QUEUED and may come back at any refresh, but every
 slot ACQUIRED means nothing is outstanding and nobody but the caller
 can change that. At timeout = UINT64_MAX it waited anyway, forever.
Evidence: hardware run 15 (2026-08-08, build 2026-08-08T20:23:02.777Z
 c2c17b9 mesa:4d90ca5), t_vk_swapchain, launched from the homebrew menu
 with no nxlink. The device was healthy — 3 images present 90 of 90
 with 89 of 89 intervals inside 10% of a refresh, 2 images 90 of 90 —
 and the test then acquired one image, failed to present it back, and
 asked for another with no deadline. The driver's own stuck warning is
 the last line in the log: "wsi_horizon: no buffer in 3000 ms and this
 swapchain still owns the window; last dequeue said 0x0000115d", and
 there is no RESULT line after it. Nothing was wrong with the device
 and nothing had taken the window; the caller held the only buffer that
 could have been released.
Upstream: no — this file is the Horizon backend itself.

Only at an infinite timeout. A finite deadline already has VK_TIMEOUT,
which is honest and lets the caller decide what to do. The result is
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR, matching what the fallback returns in the
identical situation, and it is actionable: a recreated swapchain comes
with its images back.

The caller was at fault, and that is the point. A swapchain that holds
more images than it presents is an application bug, and Vulkan permits
this call to block on it — but blocking forever without a word turns
someone's bug into a hang with no cause on screen, and this backend has
the state to say which bug it is.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 35 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index d340d5b..9bd603a 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -1285,6 +1285,41 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
       if (vk_device_is_lost(device))
          return VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST;
 
+      /* THE APPLICATION HOLDS EVERYTHING, so this is a deadlock and not
+       * a wait — and the copy fallback has said so since 0053 while
+       * this path did not. The same condition, detected on one path and
+       * not the other, is how run 15 spent its evening: a test acquired
+       * an image, failed to present it back, and then asked for another
+       * with no deadline. Nothing was wrong with the device, nothing
+       * had taken the window, and the only thing that could have freed
+       * a buffer was the caller.
+       *
+       * A slot the compositor still has is QUEUED and may come back at
+       * any refresh, so waiting for that is a real wait. Every slot
+       * ACQUIRED means there is nothing outstanding to be released and
+       * nobody but the caller can change that.
+       *
+       * Only at an infinite timeout: a finite deadline has VK_TIMEOUT,
+       * which is honest and lets the caller decide. VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_
+       * DATE_KHR here matches what the fallback already returns, and it
+       * is actionable — a recreated swapchain comes with its images
+       * back. */
+      if (timeout_ns == UINT64_MAX) {
+         bool all_acquired = true;
+         for (uint32_t i = 0; i < chain->image_count; i++) {
+            if (chain->images[i].state != WSI_HORIZON_SLOT_ACQUIRED) {
+               all_acquired = false;
+               break;
+            }
+         }
+         if (all_acquired) {
+            mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: all %u images of this swapchain are "
+                      "acquired and only a present can free one; blocking "
+                      "here would deadlock", chain->image_count);
+            return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
+         }
+      }
+
       /* An infinite wait is what the caller asked for and is not
        * shortened here. It is made observable instead: a swapchain that
        * has owned its window for this long without the compositor
-- 
